I found a stronger direction: an exact Hamiltonian-cycle count in a compactly defined graph. I’m computing candidate counts now and checking whether the statement can be made unambiguous by fixing the start vertex and orientation convention.

Tool use: Run python3 - <<'PY'

```sh
/bin/bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'
from functools import lru_cache

n=16
adj=[[False]*n for _ in range(n)]
for i in range(n):
    for j in range(n):
        if i!=j and (i^j).bit_count() in (1,2):
            adj[i][j]=True

start=0
full=(1<<n)-1
# dp[mask][v] count paths from start to v using mask, include start, distinct vertices
from collections import defaultdict
DP=[defaultdict(int) for _ in range(1<<n)]
DP[1<<start][start]=1
for mask in range(1<<n):
    if not (mask>>start)&1: continue
    for v,c in list(DP[mask].items()):
        for w in range(n):
            if not adj[v][w] or (mask>>w)&1: continue
            DP[mask|1<<w][w]+=c
count=sum(c for v,c in DP[full].items() if adj[v][start])
print(count)
PY
```

